(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-lipid-resins-ezetimibe-pcsk9-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Resins, Ezetimibe & PCSK9 Inhibitors Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Where does ezetimibe act?","choices":["The intestinal brush border","The terminal ileum","The hepatocyte enzyme","Adipose tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe selectively inhibits cholesterol absorption at the brush border membrane, blocking sterol uptake in the upper small intestine. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How do bile acid sequestrants lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["They inhibit HMG-CoA reductase","They bind bile acids in the gut","They block brush-border uptake","They activate PPAR-alpha"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bound bile acids are excreted in feces instead of recirculating, so the liver makes more bile acids from cholesterol and more LDL receptors, lowering LDL. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Cholestyramine reduces the absorption of which drug?","choices":["Warfarin","Evolocumab","Nitroprusside","Esmolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As anion exchange resins they interfere with the absorption of digoxin, warfarin, thyroxine, beta blockers and thiazide diuretics. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How should a patient prepare cholestyramine powder?","choices":["Swallow the powder dry","Dissolve it under the tongue","Mix it in water or fruit juice","Mix it with an antacid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Powders must be mixed with water or fruit juice, and a pulpy drink masks the taste. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A patient taking colestipol also takes thyroxine. How should the thyroxine be timed?","choices":["1 hour before or 4 hours after","At the same time as the resin","30 minutes after the resin","Timing does not matter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resins interfere with absorption of digoxin, warfarin, thyroxine, beta blockers and thiazide diuretics; giving the drug 1 hour before or 4 hours after the resin avoids it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A patient with a fasting triglyceride of 600 mg/dL needs cholesterol treatment. Which class is contraindicated?","choices":["Fibric acid derivatives","Nicotinic acid (niacin)","HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors","Bile acid sequestrants"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides above 400 mg/dL are an absolute contraindication to resins (above 200 mg/dL is relative), because they can raise VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production and triglycerides. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient planning a pregnancy needs LDL (low-density lipoprotein) lowering. Which class is approved in pregnancy?","choices":["Statins","Bile acid sequestrants","Fibrates","Statin with niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins are not absorbed and are approved in pregnancy, whereas pregnancy is a listed contraindication for statins and for fibrates. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What limits ezetimibe's systemic exposure?","choices":["Extensive CYP3A4 metabolism","Enterohepatic recirculation","Rapid renal excretion","It is not absorbed at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe and its active glucuronide metabolite circulate enterohepatically, returning the drug to its site of action and limiting systemic exposure. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Why are bile acid sequestrants avoided when triglycerides are high?","choices":["They may raise triglycerides","They cause gallstones","They lower HDL sharply","They damage the liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Resins may increase VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production, raising triglycerides; on the lipid comparison, resins show no change or a rise in triglycerides. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How do PCSK9 inhibitors lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["They inhibit cholesterol synthesis","They bind bile acids in the gut","They activate PPAR-alpha in liver","They keep LDL receptors active longer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. PCSK9 processes hepatic LDL receptors; blocking it keeps the receptors active for longer, lowering LDL concentrations by 43 to 58%. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Above what triglyceride level is a bile acid sequestrant a relative contraindication?","choices":["1000 mg/dL","200 mg/dL","150 mg/dL","100 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides above 200 mg/dL are a relative contraindication; above 400 mg/dL, or familial dysbetalipoproteinemia, is absolute. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What risk increases when ezetimibe is combined with a fibrate?","choices":["Hyperkalemia","Cutaneous flushing","Cholelithiasis","Angioedema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibric acid derivatives with ezetimibe increase hepatobiliary side effects, leading to cholelithiasis and myopathies. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does ezetimibe lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["It inhibits hepatic cholesterol synthesis","It binds bile acids in the terminal ileum","It blocks PCSK9 in the circulation","It blocks intestinal cholesterol absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Less cholesterol reaches the liver, so hepatic LDL receptor expression rises and atherogenic particles carry less cholesterol. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which description fits alirocumab?","choices":["Oral bile acid-binding resin","Oral HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","Injectable monoclonal antibody","Oral cholesterol absorption blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alirocumab and evolocumab are injectable-only monoclonal antibodies, and they are expensive. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 60"}]};
